xt as you make  other calls to downstream services  within your environment  that context can also be shared within  the application for functional level  calls  but this context propagation concept  actually goes beyond distributed tracing  for example i probably want to share  metadata between my different data  sources  and i can do that through the  correlation context  specification information in addition i  need a way to actually share that  between my apis and sdks and the  propagators is how we do that in the  open telemetry project  there are links to all this information  if you're really interested in learning  the specifics of it  but this is really the magic that glues  all the information together and makes  it extremely useful when you want to  actually query out and get insights from  them  so let's talk a little bit about getting  started with the open symmetry project  the easiest way to do that is to really  start talking about the architecture  so you can see here we have a host  there's an application running on it  and maybe we have one or even multiple  backends we want to send our data to  how would we get started with open  telemetry  well first we're going to go ahead and  deploy the opensymmetry collector as an  agent  the reason why we do this is kind of  twofold one is it can go ahead and grab  the system metrics for you automatically  and send that to the backend or back  ends of your choice  secondarily if i actually leverage the  open symmetry collector as an agent with  the open telemetry client libraries  then it's automatically configured for  me thanks to the use of the otop  protocol that is built into the open  symmetry project  so if i deploy the open symmetry client  library and an agent is already running  there  the traffic will be received both  metrics and traces  eventually logs and all that data can  then be sent to one or more backends  based on configuration rules that i  write in the collector itself  this provides flexibility it also also  offloads some of the responsibility from  the application itself  things like buffering and retry  encryption compression  all that can actually be offloaded from  the application which would consume  resources  and actually be handled in a single  language in the case of the collector it  is go  one way and be handled across my entire  fleet  another deployment architecture is  actually to have the collector running  as a standalone service  that would be typically in the same data  center or region i would deploy one or  more of these collector instances  and usually it's the agent that will  forward to the collector  now the idea here is maybe i want to  limit the number of egress points i have  in my environment so i don't want all  the agents to talk to the internet to  send to my vendor's backend  maybe i want to consolidate the api keys  maybe i want bigger buffer or retry  cues and so i can offload this to a set  of collectors  that i can scale up or out as needed  there are different use cases for this  another one would be around tracing  maybe i want to do tail based sampling  that typically doesn't follow an agent  model you typically need stand-alone  collectors in order to achieve that  and the open symmetry collector is  actually a single binary that can be  deployed as either an agent or as a  standalone service and is very  configurable depending on your use cases  so next i want to talk about auto  instrumentation because  if you've never got started with  instrumentation before it may seem like  a lot to actually get going  and really open symmetry is trying to  make this be as easy as possible  and then later on you can get to manual  instrumentation if you want to add your  own custom  metric or tracing information your own  metadata and enhance what's available  out of the box  the idea with auto instrumentation is  it's basically run time parameter  changes to your application  not code changes so it's very easy to  get started  here's an example of a java application  let's say i have  a java call to a jar the jar that i've  written that runs my application  all i need to do is go ahead and define  a java agent parameter  and point to the open symmetry java  agent all jar file  in addition here i've gone ahead and set  an environmental variable which will set  the service name so i know what this  service actually is and how it will be  represented in the backend that i'm  forwarding that traffic to  that's it i then just fire up my  application and i can go ahead and get  rich trace data automatically for any  supported library  and there is broad support for this  today here's an example in the case of  java of  all the different libraries and versions  that are supported now this slide is  probably already out of date because new  ones are being added every single day  so be it go ahead and take a look at  this and feel free to contribute  additional ones  but it provides broad support and really  can get you started with distributed  tracing information  with little development involvement in  order to get you started  now with that i'd like to show you a  demo of how you can actually get started  with both the open telemetry collector  and with java auto instrumentation okay  let's go ahead and fire up the demo  application  while it starts up here i'll explain to  you what's going on  first we're going to be leveraging the  spring pet clinic microservices  example application it's all based on  java  and it's actually multiple different  microservices that communicate with one  another  as you can see they have a picture on  the repository on  what it looks like from an architectural  perspective  and the applications are actually not  instrumented by default  so our goal here is going to be to add  open telemetry  java auto instrumentation to the java  micro services  and to have them report to an open  telemetry collector  which can then send to the back end or  back ends of  your choice so what i've done is i've  actually modified  this repository i've added a  collector.yaml  which will go ahead and define how we  want the openslimentry collector to be  configured  in this case i'm basically just  accepting over the  otlp protocol and defining an endpoint  where i can actually send  traffic to a zipkin server and  this only needs to be defined once in  addition uh  basically how that spring pet clinic  micro services example app works is  through a docker compose file  so each of the microservices is a  different docker container  i've gone ahead and made the following  modification  basically i've enabled the java agent  runtime parameter which you pass first  right after the java command  and i'm pointing it to the open  telemetry java agent all jar file  and then i am defining a service name  via an environmental variable  so in this case we have the discovery  service and i've repeated that for every  single microservice that's in the docker  compose  you'll see customers visits vets  finally i have defined an open telemetry  collector  that actually gets spun up as well so  that these microservices can all report  to a locally running open symmetry  collector  that uses the collector.yaml file that i  just showed  for each of the micro services we have a  docker file  and in that docker file we will go ahead  and get the jar file from the open  symmetry project  so that we can embed it at runtime as a  parameter  and have it do by code manipulation of  our application  so that's about it and then what ends up  happening is  this microservices app gets fired up and  starts reporting traffic to an open  symmetry collector you can actually see  here  log lines of different spans already  being reported into the system  so just by firing it up we haven't even  accessed the ui yet you can see the data  is being generated and being sent to the  back ends of my choice  so i will go ahead and start up the pet  clinic application  you can see it's here we can go ahead  and look at let's say  any owners that are in the system any  veterinarians that are in the system  we can go ahead and add additional  information as well  you actually interact with the system in  multiple different ways and the net  result here is  in the case of zipkin we can go ahead  and  look for any traces that are coming into  the system  we can do this by service name so for  example maybe i'll go ahead and go take  a look at  discovery service and see that there are  actually traces coming into the system  and we can actually drill into these and  see  a variety of different calls are being  made and associated  metadata we can actually see this is  coming from the open telemetry project  as well now i've actually modified the  collector to also send to  the signalfx backend so here's another  example where again the data is being  sent to two different backends so i can  actually compare or i can send different  datas to different backends  really open symmetry is looking to be  vendor agnostic and provide a generic  way of handling instrumentation for you  so you can do it once and not have to  worry about it afterwards  so exact same data here as you can see  you get a visual topology  this basically represents what we saw in  the architecture diagram  of the github repo and as you can see i  made no  java changes in order to get this  instrumentation and actually  get some rich insights both from a  visual topology perspective  as well as my red metrics request error  and duration  and any other metadata that's associated  with it so that's a very quick demo on  how you can get started  with open telemetry both the auto  instrumentation  and the collector and without making any  code modifications only runtime  parameters were able to get some rich  telemetry data out of our application  thanks for the demo steve as i mentioned  in the beginning  there are already companies and groups  that are using open telemetry in  production services today  google is one of them starting back in  january we actually deployed the open  telemetry collector  into instances of google kubernetes  engine versions one point one six and  above  uh the open telemetry collector since  then has formed the backbone of the  metrics pipeline inside of gke  and replaced a number of deprecated  components like heapster  similarly we'll be using open telemetry  for cloud ops customers particularly  cloud monitoring and cloud trace  to replace the existing open  census-based sdks that they use to  capture  application metrics custom metrics and  distributed traces  next we're going to be using open  telemetry inside of google compute  engine  we have a new windows system monitoring  agent that's actually based on the open  telemetry collector  using a lot of the system monitoring  system metrics capabilities they talked  about during the introduction  beyond this we'll be using the collector  to replace our  linux monitoring agents uh probably  later this year or late or early next  year  and that will bring in the same  capabilities that we're bringing to  windows right now  at that point i think effectively all of  our metrics and tracing stack  will have been moved over to open  telemetry at least when it comes to  capturing data from customers  applications  at splunk we're also leveraging open  telemetry extensively  we basically have this broken up into  two different categories of work  internally we want to actually consume  and leverage open telemetry as much as  possible  so in the case of signal effects and  omniscient all of the infrastructure  today  is leveraging the open symmetry  collector in order to collect both  metrics and trace information  we're actually actively underway moving  to open telemetry client libraries  as well in order to get rich insights  and move to an open source  vendor agnostic and open standard way of  gathering telemetry data from our own  environments  externally we are active contributors to  the project we're actually maintainers  of the collector  as well as the java auto instrumentation  and we're working very closely with the  community to donate some of the signalfx  open source components around the smart  agent  gateway and other auto instrumentation  semantic conventions we've been working  on  finally we're about to get kicked off  with the net auto instrumentation sig so  if you're interested in that and really  want to get involved we'd love to  have your help all right next up let's  hear from francis about how  shopify is leveraging open telemetry  shopify is partway through a migration  from a custom trace collection  infrastructure to one based heavily  around open telemetry  in addition shopify is one of the  maintainers of the open telemetry ruby  client library  at present our architecture looks like  this  we have a large number of trace services  written in several languages and  instrumented in different ways  some of these are externally developed  such as core dns and nginx  some use our custom instrumentation and  export in its custom format  some exporting jaeges format some in  open census  some in zipkin  the open telemetry collector is the core  of our architecture  we add a couple of components to it in  particular a receiver for our custom  trace format and a custom exporter  our use cases for the collector include  pii reduction  down sampling for cost control adding  tags required by vendors  renaming spans to control cardinality  and fixing instrumentation flaws  here's an example of pii reduction in  the collector focusing mainly on  database queries attached to spans by  the instrumentation  but also with app specific redaction at  the top we redact any  database statement tags unless the span  also explicitly indicates that no pii is  present  lower down we identified the pii can  leak into the jawbox tag for this  service  so we explicitly redact those tags  so you just heard from a cloud provider  google a vendor  splunk and an end user shopify about  different experiences  leveraging open telemetry as you can see  there is broad support and broad  interest in this topic in fact open  telemetry has the  second most contributions in all of cncf  behind only kubernetes  that's pretty exciting and we would love  to have you come join us  you can get involved in the conversation  on gitter you can come join a special  interest group depending on which area  you care most about  and there are tons of great issues both  with help wanted  and good first issue labels it to get  you started  please come join the community please  come join the discussion as morgan  mentioned we're just getting started  with the logging specification  so if you're interested in logging now's  a great time to get involved in that as  well  we would love to have you be part of the  solution to this important problem for  the industry as a whole  and with that thank you so much for  joining us we really appreciate it and  we hope to see you in the open symmetry  community soon  you
 

